\f1\fs22 That evening, Tony joined Sarah and Kathy for dinner. With mouth agape, he took the middle seat on the long side of the table at Sarah\'92s right, opposite Kathy who disappeared from view as soon as Tony sat down. Between them, on the table, sat castles and moats of sugar, fat and calories, all piled precariously on top of unopened casserole dishes, pots, jars, and take-away bags. A rock-pile of Fool\'92s Gold sandwiches lay at irregular angles on a plate the size of a bird bath, with bacon, peanut butter and banana mash oozing from their load-bearing sourdough. At the other end of the table, an equally imposing pile of hot dogs and sauerkraut rested with the gravity of a medieval fortress on a chessboard. To Tony\'92s right, a mound of party meatballs steamed like a peat bog. Four huge pitchers of black cherry pop sparkled like mirror balls in a disco. A leaning tower of pineapple and pecan cakes seemed like it was harassing Tony with its glowering shadow. A pyramid of shredded coconut crumbled half hidden in the metropolis of gastronomic constructions. There was a mountain of mash potato that would have made Richard Dreyfuss call for engineers to help him model Devils Tower with it. There was more Tony could only half-guess about: a shallow bowl of \'91Just Pretend\'92 Harvard Texas Caviar; Spanish omelettes; fried pickles; sugar-glazed salmon; fried potatoes the size of cannon balls; banana pudding\cf3  in a peanut-butter-and-bacon cookie crust, topped with peanut butter and whipped cream\cf2 ; a meatloaf the size of a large cat. Tony worried there 
\f2\i could
\f1\i0  be a large cat somewhere amongst the vittles.\
