\f1\i0  were scripture translated, come alive.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony picked up a bowl and removed the lid. He examined the contents like he might a stool sample; it looked like chopped snails in a swamp. \'91What the tax is this?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Captain Denver leant over to check it. \'91Oh, that\'92s one of my momma\'92s favorite recipes. Mushrooms. Momma called it Viva Las Fungus.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is there a cheeseburger in here somewhere?\'92 Tony asked hopefully. From around Kathy\'92s side of the table, a teetering stack of burgers appeared. Tony grabbed and munched.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What the tax is this?\'92 Kathy exclaimed from somewhere on the other side of Coronary Mountain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah examined something out of Tony\'92s sight. \'91Coffee grounds and bacon fat,\'92 she told Kathy. \'91Gimme!\'92 Sarah seized a bowl, grabbed the ladle in it and shovelled gagging gobs of a dark and glistening unguent into her mouth, chewing as carefully as a starving alligator. \'91What?\'92 she demanded of Tony when she noticed his ill-concealed expression of horror. \'91I\'92ve got cravings. This parasite is draining the life from me,\'92 she said defensively.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Denver loudly flipped over a page of his notebook and scribbled, police-style.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Get rid of it,\'92 Kathy said, her words muffled though the organic mass before they reached Tony\'92s ears.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah suddenly looked meek and compliant, an aspect Tony had never seen in her before, as if she was contemplating some great shame. \'91Abortions are for animals and the Cloudborne,\'92 she said, assembling a manufactured asperity. \'91An Airborne respects the sanctity of life,\'92 she added, as if by rote.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah, right,\'92 Kathy answered. \'91The higher the Airborne, the more scratch marks on her cervix.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked at her, horrified.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Surely, you won\'92t take Little Clinton away from \'85 us,\'92 Tony asked. In the absence of an answer, Tony felt his anxiety rinsing away. He imagined he saw a glow around Sarah\'92s tummy where the baby grew, and heard wondrous words of a world where religious tyranny, political corruption, and oppression (which Tony was becoming more convinced were priests of the same doctrine) was conquered by science and reason. For a more than a few moments, he was lost in reverie as he arranged new verses in his head, inspired, he was sure, by Little Clinton. Somehow, Little Clinton would prevail; Tony knew he would.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91She was raped,\'92 said a Christmas-tree-sized croquembouche with Kathy\'92s voice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, 
\f2\i raped
\f1\i0  is a little strong, isn\'92t it?\'92 Tony asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Typical man!\'92 accused the mutant puffs of 
\f2\i choux
\f1\i0  pastry soaked in caramel. \'91Always minimising violence against women!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stared Tony down. \'91They drugged me then committed an unwanted and invasive procedure on me.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah, but you gotta admit the drugs were great,\'92 Tony said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Were they?\'92 asked Kathy\'92s voice in the pastry puffs, with the singular undivided interest only a French dessert could muster.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He\'92s a mutant. He could be hideous,\'92 Sarah complained, as if ugliness ranked in horror somewhere between nuclear holocaust and\'97worse\'97a basket of muffins from Alexander Koalemos.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What are you talking about?\'92 Tony asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s synthetic DNA attached to the child\'92s DNA,\'92 said croquembouche Kathy. \'91I don\'92t know what he will turn out like.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah scrunched her face looked away at a detached spot on the floor.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Come, on, the Elvi worship you. You\'92re safe here. Where\'92s the harm in giving them one little baby?\'92 Tony shuddered inside as he contemplated the abject loneliness of a world without Little Clinton and the inspiration he brought him, as well as the respect of the Elvi. Tony had never been respected before.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Croquembouche-Kathy snorted and glared at Tony. \'91Seems like it\'92s the men who are campaigning for the little monster the most,\'92 she said dryly. \'91They never credit women for enduring the difficulty of bearing children. Then there\'92s all the discrimination, from bosses and colleagues. No one will ever say anything outright, to your face, but you will see evidence of it piling up everyday. When they\'92re not trying to fuck you or kill you, men are really funny.\'92 Kathy wasn\'92t laughing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony suspected the croquembouche was speaking from bitter experience, a working mother-pastry in a man-pastry\'92s world.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was flooded with memories of her old life: a professional career, able to do as she pleased, men and women fawning on her where ever she went. Tax! She was invincible \'85 once. \'91Perhaps I should get rid of it,\'92 she conceded.
